! mcp_bootstrap_p193.strap -- MCP printed page 193: the "get offline /
! bad label" arm of the tape-label routine, the three operator-message
! DD cards it prints, and the "take the unit off" (J TAKF) loop that
! unloads the reel and waits for a new one.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.200.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LG000215   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  193".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 221542. <<<
! Six digits, printed after the word LOCATION at the right-hand end of
! the heading line, clear of the dashed rule.  That is LINE 1'S
! location word (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and line 1
! here IS a located row -- the case the rule predicts to be NUMBERED
! rather than blank.  Line 1's own LOCATION column reads 221542.00.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIFTY-TWO PRINTED LINES, AND ALL FIFTY-TWO COMPILE.  There is not
! one comment card on this page, not one banner, no SLC, no SYN, no
! reservation of any kind.  Four of the fifty-two are (A*)DD
! character cards (lines 22-25); the other forty-eight are machine
! instructions, half-word null operations, CNOP and VF.
!
! >>> SO verify_mcp_page.py REPORTS "8 symbols, 48 listing rows" AND
! >>> NOTHING IS MISSING. <<<  52 = 48 rows that print a BINARY
! OUTPUT, each with its own !CHECK, PLUS the four (A*)DD cards,
! which print no binary at all and therefore cannot carry one.
! What stands in for their checks is set out under THE COUNTER
! CHAIN and again in the note at the foot of this file.
!
! The page is CONTIGUOUS from 221542.00 to 221617.00 -- no hole,
! nothing skipped, every row's width accounted for -- and then blank
! paper to the foot of the form.
!
!   lines  1-21   the bad-label arm.  BB,SOWNER($11),JITSMC splits
!                 MCP's own unit off from a problem program's; the
!                 reel name is picked up with LF(BU,30,6), a scratch
!                 tape branches away to J ITS SC, and otherwise the
!                 reel number is edited into the message with
!                 SF(BU,40),JSPRNO.  J GETOF tests S CH OP and the
!                 page then prints the message through SPRIME with
!                 three parameter half words (,DSPR / ,0($1) / ,1.0).
!                 BB,SL,JPREOJ then picks the PP-level exit
!                 (SIC,SDISIC / B,SDISP, error control) or the
!                 MCP-level exit J PREOJ (SPRIME again with ,DABEOJ,
!                 then B,KSUPP).  Line 21's CNOP rounds the counter
!                 to the word the first message needs.
!   lines 22-25   four (A*)DD operator-message cards at EIGHT BITS a
!                 character.  J LBMES is the message head; J SPRNO is
!                 the card whose first five characters the SF on line
!                 5 overwrites with the reel number.
!   lines 26-29   J ITS SC, the scratch-tape path: SIC,KCONVR /
!                 B,KCONV computes the channel-unit number, VF,JREPCU
!                 and LVI,$2,JREPSC set up the "replace reel" message.
!   lines 30-46   J TAKF, the take-the-unit-off loop: LX,$0,JUNLDK
!                 loads the unload command, SIC,KKRET / B,KIOREJ
!                 issues it, LVI,$1,$+1.0 / B,JSEOPT waits for
!                 termination, LF(BU,1),JIOBSY and SF(BU,1),JWAIT
!                 move the busy flag, SIC,SCOMIC / B,SCOMM tells the
!                 operator (with ,0.0($2) / ,8.0), SIC,KUNRR /
!                 B,KKLOOP releases, and TI,3,JNEWXR,$1 saves three
!                 index registers.
!   line  47      J NEWT, B,$ -- the wait loop p.189 line 2 SICs.
!   lines 48-52   J ITS MC, the MCP-unit path: the same
!                 SIC,KCONVR / B,KCONV pair, then VF,JRCTCU /
!                 LVI,$2,JREPCT for the "appropriate MCP reel"
!                 message, and B,JTAKF back into the loop.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SPAN, ESTABLISHED FROM THE PAGE AND NOT FROM THE SURVEY
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIRST 221542.00 (line 1), LAST 221616.40 (line 52), FIFTY-TWO
! LINES, all located.  The counter leaves the page at 221617.00.
!
! The brief no longer quotes survey endpoints, so all three numbers
! come from the page's own ink:
!
!   * the LINE column runs 1..52 with no gap (read at 800 dpi);
!   * the CARD SERIAL column runs LG000215..LG000265 -- fifty-ONE
!     distinct serials for fifty-TWO lines, because LG000258 is
!     printed TWICE, on lines 44 and 45 (confirmed at 1200 dpi;
!     see THE DUPLICATE SERIAL below).  Nothing fell off either end:
!     the header's own CARD ID is LG000215, which is line 1's
!     serial, and the serial column stops with line 52;
!   * the counter chain closes digit for digit (see below).
!
! For the record, strap/mcp_ocr.py reads 15 of the 52 rows and its
! last is line 41 at 221610.40 -- eleven lines short of the page's
! end, exactly the under-read the brief describes.  It is not quoted
! anywhere in this deck as evidence.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE COUNTER CHAIN, AND WHY THE FOUR DD CARDS ARE THE HARD PART
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Forty-eight of the fifty-two rows have a width that is fixed by
! their operation: a half word or a full word.  Those chain
! trivially and every one of their locations is checked below.
!
! THE FOUR (A*)DD CARDS DO NOT.  Their width is EIGHT BITS A
! CHARACTER (printed p.184 established this and its four counts
! closed on its own printed locations), so the character count --
! including the trailing blanks, which are invisible -- is the only
! unknown on the page.  Here it is pinned FOUR TIMES, each time by
! the PRINTED LOCATION of the next card:
!
!   221557.00 + 38 chars x 8 = 304 bits -> 221563.60   line 22->23
!   221563.60 + 22 chars x 8 = 176 bits -> 221566.40   line 23->24
!   221566.40 + 36 chars x 8 = 288 bits -> 221573.00   line 24->25
!   221573.00 + 40 chars x 8 = 320 bits -> 221600.00   line 25->26
!
! and 221563.60, 221566.40, 221573.00 and 221600.00 are what lines
! 23, 24, 25 and 26 PRINT.  Four unknown counts, four printed
! locations, no slack.  (221563.60 is NOT a half-word boundary and
! is not supposed to be; an early reading of this deck took it for
! "221563.00" and the arithmetic refused to close, which is how the
! error was caught.)
!
! >>> AND LINE 24'S COUNT IS PINNED A SECOND TIME, BIT-EXACTLY,
! >>> WITHOUT USING THE CHAIN AT ALL. <<<  Line 5 is
! "SF(BU,40),JSPRNO" and prints 221566.40 in its first half word.
! A VFL OPERAND READS ITS OPERAND TO THE BIT (workflow, "WHAT THE
! ORACLE CANNOT SEE" item 1), so that row is a reading of J SPRNO's
! bit address and it agrees with the location line 24 prints.  That
! closes lines 22 and 23's counts from the far end independently of
! the counter arriving from line 21.
!
! >>> THIRD READING: THE TERMINATOR'S OWN PRINTED COLUMN. <<<  The
! prefix "(A*)DD(BU)," is eleven characters, so the "*" must stand
! at column 11+n.  Measured off the 1200-dpi render (column 0 taken
! from the "(" of line 22, character pitch measured at 118.5 px):
!
!     line 22   predicted 49   measured 48.5
!     line 23   predicted 33   measured 33.0
!     line 24   predicted 47   measured 46.8
!     line 25   predicted 51   measured 51.0
!
! >>> LINE 25'S FORTY BLANKS ARE DERIVED FROM THIS PAGE. <<<
! 221573.00 -> 221600.00 is five whole words = 320 bits = 40
! characters, and the terminator column agrees.  printed p.184 line
! 34 happens to carry forty blanks as well; that is a COINCIDENCE
! and is not the evidence.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, BACKWARD AND FORWARD
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BACKWARD.  printed p.192's deck predicted, in writing and before
! this page was opened, that p.193's first located row would be
! 221542.00 and not 221542.40, because its line 51
! "J NEWTE TI,3,$1,JNEWXR" is a FULL word at 221541.00.  >>> THE
! PREDICTION HOLDS. <<<  Line 1 prints 221542.00 and the heading
! row's LOCATION field prints 221542.
!
! p.192's prediction (2) -- "its first card serial is LG000214" --
! is OFF BY ONE in the harmless direction: LG000214 is p.192's OWN
! last serial, and this page opens at LG000215.  The run is
! contiguous across the break, which is a third, independent
! witness for the join and the one p.192 flagged as the weak one.
!
! FORWARD.  Line 52 is "B,JTAKF", a half word at 221616.40, so the
! counter leaves this page at 221617.00.  TWO rows -- this page's
! line 46 "TI,3,JNEWXR,$1" (first half 221617.00) and printed
! p.192's line 51 "TI,3,$1,JNEWXR" (second half 221617.06), the
! same instruction with its operands swapped -- place J NEWXR at
! word 221617, and p.192's deck records the volume OCR carrying a
! three-word reservation there.  So the next page almost certainly
! OPENS with that reservation.  See PREDICTIONS below.
!
! >>> THIS DECK'S SLC IS NOT PRINTED -- AND THE ORIGIN IS CHECKED
! >>> ANYWAY, MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. <<<  The page carries no SLC
! card, so the deck writes a hard "SLC,(8)221542.0".  A hard SLC
! cannot disagree with the arriving counter.  But TWO landed decks
! SYN labels THIS page defines, with bare (8) literals rather than
! aliases, so the merge compares them:
!
!   decks/mcp_bootstrap_p184.strap:524  JGETOF SYN, (8)221546.40
!   decks/mcp_bootstrap_p189.strap:445  JNEWT  SYN, (8)221614.0
!
! and this page prints J GETOF at 221546.40 (line 7) and J NEWT at
! 221614.00 (line 47).  THE INJECTION WAS RUN (brief: "believe the
! result, not the grep", after p.189 found the false positive):
!
!   origin as printed  SLC,(8)221542.0   -> MERGE OK
!                         832 defined, 62 external, 0 unaccounted words
!   origin shifted     SLC,(8)221542.40  -> REFUSED:
!        FAIL JGETOF: printed p.184 SYNs bit 4774304,
!                     printed p.193 places it at bit 4774336
!
! >>> SO THE ORIGIN OF THIS PAGE IS CHECKED, NOT ASSERTED. <<<
!
! >>> BUT ONLY ONE OF THE TWO WITNESSES IS DOING THE CHECKING, AND
! >>> THE REASON IS A NEW FALSE-POSITIVE MECHANISM THE BRIEF DOES
! >>> NOT LIST. <<<  The merge stops at the first disagreement, so I
! reconciled p.184's SYN WITH the shift in a staged copy and re-ran
! to see whether J NEWT would then refuse.  IT DID NOT -- the merge
! came back OK.  Measured directly, assembling this deck at three
! origins and reading the symbol table:
!
!     origin        J GETOF (line 7)   J NEWT (line 47)
!     221542.0      221546.40          221614.00
!     221542.40     221547.00          221614.00   <- UNMOVED
!     221543.0      221547.40          221615.00
!
! >>> LINE 21'S "CNOP" ABSORBS A HALF-WORD ORIGIN ERROR. <<<  CNOP
! rounds the counter UP TO A WORD BOUNDARY, so at the printed origin
! it stands at 221556.40 and emits one NOP, while at an origin one
! half word later it stands at 221557.00, emits NOTHING, and the
! shift disappears.  Everything from line 22 onward -- J LBMES,
! J SPRNO, J ITS SC, J NEWT, J ITS MC -- lands at the SAME address
! either way.
!
! So of this page's two landed witnesses, only J GETOF (line 7,
! BEFORE the CNOP) can see a half-word error; J NEWT (line 47,
! AFTER it) sees only whole-word errors, and it does see those.
! This is a DIFFERENT false positive from the one printed p.189
! found -- p.189's was SYN aliasing, this one is a realigning
! statement inside the page -- and it is the same shape as the
! workflow's note that "on printed p.98 a wrong SLC still produced
! the right address for the next row, because a half-word
! instruction realigned the counter".  >>> IF A PAGE CONTAINS A
! CNOP, AN ORIGIN INJECTION MUST BE RUN AT A FULL WORD AS WELL AS
! AT A HALF, OR IT WILL REPORT "CHECKED" ON THE STRENGTH OF LABELS
! THAT CANNOT MOVE. <<<
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE DUPLICATE CARD SERIAL -- AN OBSERVATION, NOT A RULE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LG000258 is printed on TWO consecutive lines, 44 and 45, which are
! "SIC,KUNRR" and "B,KKLOOP" -- a SIC and its mandatory half-word
! branch.  Read at 1200 dpi with the neighbours LG000257 and
! LG000259 in the same crop; the two 258s are character-for-
! character identical and neither is a damaged 9 or 3.
!
! printed p.192 records the same shape at its lines 32/33 (LG000195
! on a SIC/branch pair).  THAT IS NOT ENOUGH FOR A RULE, AND THIS
! PAGE REFUTES THE OBVIOUS ONE: six other SIC/branch pairs here --
! lines 8/9, 15/16, 17/18, 26/27, 31/32 and 40/41 -- carry DISTINCT
! consecutive serials.  So "a SIC and its branch share a card"
! is false on this page six times out of seven.  Recorded and
! moved past; it costs nothing because the serial column is not an
! input to anything the assembler does.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS, AND WHAT SETTLED EACH ONE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) "S CH OP" (line 7).  The middle glyph is damaged and reads C,
!     H, K or F at 1600 dpi.  Settled by the MANUALS, not the ink:
!       grep -ho 'SC[A-Z]OP[A-Z0-9]*' docs/ocr/*.txt | sort | uniq -c
!         20 SCHOP        2 SCNOP
!     and SCHOP is landed in sixteen decks as SYN(BU,1), 0.26
!     ("-CHAN OPERATING", printed p.99 line 22).  The row's own
!     binary clinches it: printed p.135 line 41 prints EXACTLY
!     "000000.32 8A  001000.00 F0" for "CM0000(BU,1),SCHOP($10)",
!     which is this row's word for this row's word.
! (2) "CM0000" (line 7) prints as CMOCOO -- five round glyphs, four
!     of them the operand digits.  A CM mnemonic's four digits are
!     all the same character, and the operation set has CM0000 and
!     CM1111; the binary settles which (CM1111 prints 001000.36,
!     CM0000 prints 001000.00, and this row prints 001000.00).
! (3) Line 4's byte C2 is NOT a damaged 02.  printed p.192 line 36
!     measured "BRZ,1.32($1) -> 000001.74 C3" and recorded that the
!     byte really is C3; C3 = C2 OR the $1 index, so an unindexed
!     BRZ is C2.  Handed to the assembler as printed and it agrees.
! (4) Line 46's byte A0.  At 1200 dpi the low glyph is open and
!     reads AC; it is A0, and NOT because of its shape.  The second
!     operand is "$1" with no parenthesised index, so i2 = 0; and
!     printed p.192's line 51 is the SAME INSTRUCTION with its two
!     operands swapped and its byte was re-read at 1200 dpi there
!     for exactly this reason and came out A0.  strap_asm emits A0.
! (5) Line 3's "LF(BU,30,6)" -- the "0" of 30 is a round glyph in a
!     DECIMAL field, so the octal-field argument does NOT apply.
!     It is settled by the BINARY instead: the second half word
!     prints 036600, i.e. length 0o36 = 30 and byte size 6, against
!     line 38's 001000 for "LF(BU,1)".
! (6) "JUNLDK" (line 30) -- D or O, which the brief warns is not in
!     the spelling checker's confusable set.  Settled by the volume
!     OCR used as an INDEX of another page: JUNLDK appears three
!     times and JUNLOK zero, and one of the three is the DEFINING
!     card at 221721.00 on a later printed page
!     ("JUNLDK CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)137.0 -UNLOAD COMMAND").
! (7) Every ".CC"/".C0"/"C0C000" in the LOCATION and the octal
!     halves of the BINARY column is 0 by the octal-field argument
!     (rung 5): those fields cannot hold a C.  This page drops ink
!     on round glyphs constantly and that fact is used ONLY where
!     the field is octal.
!
! >>> AND THE PAGE CHECKS ITS OWN INDEX ENCODING SIX TIMES. <<<
! The low nibble of the printed byte is the operand's index, and
! six rows agree with six different parenthesised registers:
!   line  1  BB,SOWNER($11),...   byte 8B   -> $11
!   line  2  LV,$2,SCREEL($12)    byte 3C   -> $12
!   line  3  LF(...),.34($2)      byte 82   -> $2
!   line  7  CM0000(...),...($10) byte 8A   -> $10
!   line 11  ,0($1)               byte 81   -> $1
!   line 42  ,0.0($2)             byte 82   -> $2
! That is worth more than any single glyph call, because it is six
! independent rows agreeing on one encoding.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS PAGE DOES NOT HAVE (asked by the brief, answered once)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   * No DR and no DRZ, and no forward SLC, so NO HOLE in the
!     image is claimed or needed.  The chain is dense.
!   * No VFL IMMEDIATE at all, so this page cannot exercise the
!     "same immediate at two lengths" shape printed p.186 found.
!     The LF/SF rows carry ADDRESSES, not immediates.
!   * No I-O function pseudo-op from C28-6129 Appendix B.  The one
!     control code this page's routine issues is loaded from
!     J UNLDK, which is a CTL card on a LATER page.
!   * No duplicate LABEL (the duplicate here is a card SERIAL,
!     which the assembler never sees).
!   * No mode-only "(BU)" dds against a symbol of known length, so
!     the brief's open question about dds inheritance is not
!     settled here either.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- AND WHICH OF THEM THIS PAGE READS TO THE BIT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BIT-EXACT, because the referencing row is a SIC or a VFL operand
! or a VF (workflow: these read every bit of their operand):
!   SPRIMR  215571.00   lines  8, 17   SIC
!   SDISIC  217451.40   line  15       SIC
!   SCOMIC  215470.40   line  40       SIC
!   KCONVR  221350.00   lines 26, 48   SIC
!   KKRET   221512.40   line  31       SIC
!   KUNRR   221325.40   line  44       SIC
!   SOWNER  000000.40   line   1       BB, a 24-bit bit address
!   SL      215001.04   line  13       BB, a 24-bit bit address
!   JSPRNO  221566.40   line   5       SF, a VFL operand
!   JIOBSY  233666.04   line  38       LF, a VFL operand
!   JWAIT   233666.00   line  39       SF, a VFL operand
!   JNEWXR  221617.00   line  46       TI first operand
!   JREPCU  221674.50   line  28       VF -- a 24-bit value field
!   JRCTCU  221714.50   line  50       VF -- a 24-bit value field
!
! HALF WORD ONLY (a branch or an index operation shows bit 32 and
! nothing below it), so the ".0" in these SYNs is a CONVENTION and
! not a reading:
!   SPRIME 215570.0  SDISP 217372.0  KSUPP 216242.0  KCONV 221335.0
!   KIOREJ 221505.0  JSEOPT 221525.0 SCOMM 215470.0  KKLOOP 221321.40
!   SLABNG (16)105.0 SCREEL 8.32     SCHOP 0.26      DSPR 67.32
!   DABEOJ (8)104.0  JREPSC 221671.0 JREPCT 221711.0 JUNLDK 221721.0
!
! Every one of these but five is already landed in decks/ at the
! value written here, and the five new ones are below.
!
! >>> FIVE SYMBOLS ARE NEW TO THE CORPUS WITH THIS PAGE. <<<
! J REP CU, J REP SC, J UNL DK, J RCT CU and J REP CT are all
! FORWARD references -- 221671..221721 is past this page's end --
! and the volume OCR carries their DEFINING cards on a later
! printed page, which is the one use of the volume OCR the workflow
! blesses (an index of where a symbol is defined on some OTHER
! page).  Transcribed from that block, with my page's reading of
! each beside it:
!
!   OCR 29* 221671.00  J REPSC (IQS*)DD(BU),REPLACE T REEL ON ...
!       -> my line 29 LVI,$2 prints 221671.05, which pins the HALF
!          WORD only.  I write .0 and say so.
!   OCR 30* 221674.50  J REPCU (IQS*)DD(BU),XX UNIT X WITH A ...
!       -> my line 28 VF prints 221674.50, BIT-EXACT.  Agreement.
!   OCR 35* 221711.00  J REPCT (IQS*)DD(BU),REPLACE THE REEL ON ...
!       -> my line 51 LVI,$2 prints 221711.05, HALF WORD only.
!   OCR 36* 221714.50  J RCTCU (IQS*)DD(BU),XX UNIT X WITH ...
!       -> my line 50 VF prints 221714.50, BIT-EXACT.  Agreement.
!   OCR 38* 221721.00  J UNLDK CTL(SEOP),0.0($14),(8)137.0
!       -> my line 30 LX,$0 prints 221721.00 10, HALF WORD only.
!
! The two .50 offsets are what make J REP SC and J REP CT worth
! flagging: on that page the message cards sit at .50 as often as
! at .00, so a "convention" .0 for those two is a GUESS that my
! page cannot check.  See PREDICTIONS.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (a) >>> J REP SC's AND J REP CT's BIT OFFSETS. <<<  My page reads
!     them through LVI,$2, which shows bit 32 and nothing below it,
!     so 221671.00 and 221711.00 are CONVENTIONS here.  The volume
!     OCR of the defining page reads both as .00, but that is an
!     OCR, and the two symbols printed immediately after each of
!     them on that same page (J REP CU, J RCT CU) sit at .50.  If
!     the defining page turns out to put either at a non-zero
!     offset, THIS DECK'S SYN IS WRONG and the merge will say so.
!     I have left the convention in rather than guess .50, and
!     named it here so it is not mistaken for a reading.
! (b) The bit offsets of SPRIME, SDISP, KSUPP, KCONV, KIOREJ,
!     JSEOPT, SCOMM and KKLOOP are likewise unobservable from this
!     page; all eight are landed at these values by other decks,
!     so this page agrees with them rather than establishing them.
! (c) THE TRAILING BLANKS INSIDE THE DD CARDS ARE A COUNT, NOT A
!     READING.  I know line 24 ends with four blanks and line 25
!     carries forty, because the arithmetic and the terminator
!     column both say so.  I cannot see them.  If the real cards
!     carried, say, a tab character or a different pad, the count
!     would still be right and the CONTENT of those bytes would be
!     wrong -- and nothing on this page could tell.
! (c2) >>> AND THE TWO SUMS ARE CHECKED WHILE THE TWO SPLITS ARE
!     NOT, EQUALLY. <<<  !SYM J SPRNO pins 38+22 = 60 characters and
!     the !CHECK at 221600.00 pins 36+40 = 76.  Those two totals are
!     re-run by every build.  The SPLIT inside each pair -- 38
!     against 22, 36 against 40 -- rests on the PRINTED LOCATIONS of
!     lines 23 and 25, and those rows have no label and no binary,
!     so nothing in the !SYM/!CHECK vocabulary can express them.
!     THE SYNTHETIC-LABEL PROBE RECORDED AT THE FOOT OF THIS FILE
!     IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT WITNESS TO THE SPLITS: the assembler is
!     counting the characters I typed, so it cannot disagree with
!     me about how many I typed.  What it does check is that my
!     counts reproduce the two PRINTED locations, which is the same
!     evidence stated twice.  The genuinely independent leg is the
!     terminator column (48.5 / 33.0 / 46.8 / 51.0 against 49 / 33 /
!     47 / 51), and that rests on a pitch I measured by hand at
!     118.5 px.  If a later reader wants the splits nailed harder,
!     the thing to do is re-measure that pitch from a row of known
!     text.
! (d) Whether the duplicate LG000258 means the two cards were
!     punched as one, or the serial was simply repunched, is not
!     answerable from a listing.  Six counterexamples on this page
!     say it is not a convention; that is all I have.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER NOTES (report, do not work around silently)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> NOTHING ON THIS PAGE WAS REFUSED AND NOTHING WAS
! >>> SUBSTITUTED IN A STATEMENT. <<<  Every one of the fifty-two
! statements was handed to strap_asm exactly as printed and all
! fifty-two were accepted on the first run, including the four
! (A*)DD cards, both VF rows, CNOP, the six bare-comma null
! operations and TI,3,JNEWXR,$1.
!
! >>> AND LINE 7 IS A NEW MEASUREMENT THE BRIEF DOES NOT COVER. <<<
! The brief says a blanked name "with ANYTHING after it" is still
! refused and gives "K WS.19" and "K GOOF+.32" as the cases.  Those
! are EXPRESSION suffixes.  An INDEX is not:
!
!   SCHOP   SYN(BU,1), 0.26
!   JGETOF  CM0000(BU,1),S CH OP($10)      -> ACCEPTED
!
! and it compiles 000000.32 8A 001000.00 F0, the word the page
! prints.  So the rule is narrower than the brief states: a blanked
! name followed by a PARENTHESISED INDEX resolves.  Worth a line in
! the brief; measured here, not assumed.
!
! THE ONE SUBSTITUTION ON THIS PAGE IS IN A LABEL FIELD, not a
! statement: line 7's NAME is printed "J GETOF" and the deck writes
! "JGETOF", because a blank in the NAME field really is refused.
! Every landed deck in the corpus closes up its label field for the
! same reason, and the merge agrees with p.184's SYN of this very
! label, so nothing is lost but the blank itself.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> FALSIFIABLE PREDICTIONS FOR printed p.194 (PDF p.201) <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   (1) >>> ITS FIRST LOCATED ROW IS 221617.00. <<<  Line 52 is a
!       half word at 221616.40.  If p.194's first location is
!       221616.40 or 221617.40, one of us is wrong.
!   (2) Its first card serial is LG000266, and my last is LG000265.
!       WEAKER THAN (1): the brief records serials that print no
!       listing line at page breaks, so a gap would not falsify the
!       join -- a serial BELOW LG000266 would.
!   (3) Its heading-row LOCATION field reads 221617 UNLESS its line
!       1 is a banner or comment card, in which case it is blank.
!   (4) p.194 DEFINES J NEWXR as a THREE-WORD reservation at
!       221617.00.  Not my reading: printed p.192's deck records the
!       volume OCR carrying that card, and my line 46 and p.192's
!       line 51 both place the symbol at word 221617.
!   (5) p.194 does NOT define J REP SC, J REP CU, J UNL DK, J RCT CU
!       or J REP CT.  The volume OCR puts all five on a page whose
!       rows run 221662..221730, which is at least one page further
!       on; if p.194 ran that far it would have to cover sixty-odd
!       words in fifty-odd lines, which this listing never does.
!   (6) p.194 defines none of this page's eight labels (J GETOF,
!       J PREOJ, J LBMES, J SPRNO, J ITS SC, J TAKF, J NEWT,
!       J ITS MC).
!
! >>> AND ONE FOR WHOEVER TRANSCRIBES 221671..221721 <<<
!   (7) That page prints J REP SC at 221671.xx and J REP CT at
!       221711.xx.  THIS DECK ASSERTS xx = 00 FOR BOTH AND CANNOT
!       CHECK IT.  If either is .50 (as its neighbours J REP CU and
!       J RCT CU are, which THIS page reads bit-exactly), the merge
!       will refuse against this deck and this deck is the one that
!       must change.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! JOINING IT TO THE BODY IMAGE -- CALLER ACTION REQUIRED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! pp.99+ are MCP's body, a DIFFERENT assembly from the pp.15-98
! bootstrap, so this page goes in tests/check_mcp_body_image.py's
! FIRST, LAST (and its body_page_count), NOT in strap/mcp_image.py's
! MCP_IPL_PAGES.  The brief forbids this agent from writing under
! tests/, so TWO EDITS ARE LEFT TO THE CALLER, both in
! tests/check_mcp_body_image.py:
!
!     line 78   FIRST, LAST = 99, 192      ->  99, 193
!     line 99   check("body_page_count", len(pages), 93)   ->  94
!
! 94 is measured, not predicted: 99..193 is 95 numbers and
! mcp_image.NO_DECK_PAGES removes exactly one of them (100), so the
! page list this deck was merged with has 94 entries.  The merge
! itself was run from the scratchpad against that list and its
! result is in THE JOIN above.
!
! ===============================================================

